<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Varendra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barind</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Varendra is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karatoya-river">Karatoya</a> rivers, largely within the present Rajshahi Division of Bangladesh and the adjoining districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. It was the ancestral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the Pala dynasty, lost to the Kaivarta rebels in the late eleventh century and recovered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramapala">Ramapala</a>. Its higher, older alluvial ground is known as the Barind Tract.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/varendra.jpg" alt="Varendra" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaivartas_of_the_Varendras_circa_640-730_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
